	affiliations = {College of Business Administration, University of Bahrain, Zallaq, Bahrain},
	abstract = {Fuzzy logic has re-emerged as a foundational paradigm for building transparent, interpretable, and ethically governed AI in finance. However, existing scholarship remains fragmented, with limited integration of sustainability, explainability, and Shariah-aligned requirements. This study delivers the first large-scale scientometric analysis of fuzzy-finance research, covering 92,804 publications (2000-2025) across four major databases using a manually designed hybrid PRISMA-network-semantic pipeline. The analytical framework integrates citation performance metrics, modularity-based network clustering, burst detection, and hybrid topic modeling (LDA and BERTopic) to assess the methodological, interpretive, and contextual evolution of the field. The findings show a three-phase trajectory: the foundational development of fuzzy sets and MCDM models; the hybridization of fuzzy systems with neural, evolutionary, and deep-learning architectures; and a recent ethical consolidation driven by explainable AI, ESG priorities, and Islamic-finance imperatives. The study proposes the Explainable Fuzzy-Finance (X-FF) framework, integrating interpretability, ethics and contextual alignment into financial AI based on these quantitative patterns. Also, it identifies emerging research frontiers in sustainability-focused and Shariah-compliant analytics by offering a reproducible roadmap for transparent, auditable, and value-aligned intelligent finance. The findings contribute to the theoretical consolidation, methodological maturity, and practical guidance for regulators, ESG investors, Islamic banks, and institutions seeking trustworthy financial intelligence. © 2025 IEEE.},
