	abstract = {We present and validate a two-stage framework for explainable clustering of public-sector units that balances technical validity and managerial adoption. Stage 1 formalizes algorithm selection as a transparent multi-criteria decision-making (MCDM) problem that combines internal indices (Silhouette, Calinski-Harabasz, Davies-Bouldin) with stability (Adjusted Rand Index), scalability (median runtime), and interpretability. Stage 2 withholds a business outcome and conducts post-hoc validation via ANOVA/Welch and Tukey/Games-Howell. On 59,444 unit-month records from a large public financial institution, the framework selects K-means (k=7) and identifies seven organizational archetypes with statistically significant differences in financial performance. We report 20 independent runs per configuration, fixed seeds, and reproducible settings. The archetypes support targeted HR interventions and evidence-based management, empirically reinforcing Contingency Theory (fit between workforce and territorial context). The study illustrates how explainable clustering can translate large-scale administrative data into trustworthy, auditable decision support for government applications. © 2025 IEEE.},
