	affiliations = {Department of Civil Engineering, University of Hormozgan, P.O. Box 3995, Bandar Abbas, Iran; School of Civil and Environmental Engineering, UNSW, Sydney, 2052, NSW, Australia},
	abstract = {This study presents an integrated sustainability decision-support framework for incorporating recycled tire waste into green concrete (GC) for large-scale retaining walls and pavements in moderate-to-cold climates. The framework is designed to manage uncertainty, ensure reliability, and establish credible multi-criteria decision-making (MCDM), moving beyond sequential testing by interlinking three complementary components: (i) expert-driven weighting of technical, economic, and environmental criteria using the fuzzy SIWEC method, (ii) experimental evaluation of 15 GC mixtures containing crumb rubber (CR) and recycled steel fibers (SFs), and (iii) a multi-method MCDM ranking system combining five techniques (DEPART, ARLON, RAWEC, AROMAN, MARCOS), aggregated via the Copeland method for cross-validation. SIWEC was chosen for its scalability and reduced computational load in handling numerous criteria common in construction materials evaluation. The MCDM methods offer complementary advantages: DEPART captures subtle mix differences via pairwise deviation ratios; ARLON handles exponential material property with log normalization; RAWEC provides fast, transparent decisions; AROMAN’s hybrid normalization reduces unit bias (e.g., MPa, cost, kg); and MARCOS resists rank reversal through ideal/anti-ideal benchmarking. Results show that performance criteria weighting is application-specific. For retaining walls, mechanical properties dominated (57 % weight), with flexural strength most critical. For pavements, durability (37 %) and mechanical performance (42 %) were decisive, with abrasion resistance leading. This shows sustainability priorities must align with structural application's function. Mixture C5F0.5 ranked highest for retaining walls, while C0F0.5 was optimal for pavements. RAWEC correlated most with other MCDM methods for retaining walls (over 77.36 %), while DEPART showed highest consistency for pavements (exceeding 68.52 %), indicating superior ranking reliability. Despite minor inconsistencies from ARLON, most methods produced consistent rankings. By embedding expert-driven weighting, experimental data, and multi-method decision synthesis, this framework provides a replicable, generalizable, and validated pathway for sustainable GC supply chains. © 2025 Elsevier Ltd.},
