	pages = {5239 - 5254},
	doi = {10.1007/s12008-024-02130-4},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85206826050?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {Department of Mechanical Engineering, Viswajyoti College of Engineering and Technology, Kerala, Vazhakulam, Muvattupuzha, India; Department of Mechanical Engineering, Mar Athanasius College of Engineering, Kerala, Kothamangalam, India; Department of Industrial and Systems Engineering, Indian Institute of Technology, Kharagpur, India; Department of Production Engineering, Jadavpur University, West Bengal, Kolkata, India},
	abstract = {Carbon fiber-reinforced polymer (CFRP) composites, possessing some unique properties, like high specific strength, stiffness, corrosion and temperature resistance etc. have now become attractive choices in many of the aviation, automobile, marine, military, medical, construction and sports industries. But, their anisotropic and non-homogeneous structure, and low thermal conductivity make them quite difficult to machine, occasionally leading to excessive tool wear and poor surface quality. In this paper, treating spindle speed (N), feed rate (f) and depth of cut (a<sub>p</sub>) as the input parameters, 27 milling experiments are conducted on CFRP composite laminates based on Taguchi’s L<sub>27</sub> orthogonal array, and their effects on material removal rate (MRR), and two surface roughness parameters, i.e. Ra and Rz are investigated. Results of the parametric analysis are later validated using an explainable artificial intelligence technique, in the form of Shapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP), accompanied by ascertaining the most significant input parameters affecting each of the responses of the milling process. The said process is finally optimized using a multi-criteria decision making tool, i.e. technique for order of preference by similarity to ideal solution (TOPSIS), identifying an ideal combination of the milling parameters as N = 7500 rpm, f = 0.3 mm/sec and a<sub>p</sub> = 1.5 mm, resulting in simultaneous maximization of MRR and minimization of the two surface roughness parameters. This paper thus provides a new framework for parametric analysis and multi-objective optimization of milling of CFRP composite laminates resulting in higher productivity along with better surface quality. © The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Springer-Verlag France SAS, part of Springer Nature 2024.},
	author_keywords = {CFRP; Explainable artificial intelligence; Material removal rate; Milling; Multi-criteria decision making; Surface roughness; TOPSIS},
