	affiliations = {University of Texas at El Paso, Department of Industrial Manufacturing and Systems Engineering, El Paso, 79968, TX, United States},
	abstract = {In today's volatile geopolitical environment and heightened emphasis on sustainability, effective supplier selection must simultaneously handle cost, delivery risks, and ESG considerations. This paper proposes a GPT-Augmented Bayesian Reinforcement Learning (i-SUP) Framework, which integrates 1) GPT to extract real-time risk signals from unstructured text (news, social media), 2) Bayesian-BWM to capture expert uncertainty and produce robust multi-criteria weights, 3) Bayesian Belief Networks (BBNs) for continuously updated disruption probabilities, 4) Reinforcement Learning (RL) for dynamic monthly or weekly order allocation, and 5) NSGA-II for long-horizon multi-objective contract planning. By combining semantic risk detection with Bayesian updates and RL-based adaptive decision-making, i-SUP dynamically adjusts to emergent risks (e.g., tariffs, labor unrest), while concurrently balancing ESG imperatives and cost efficiency. Empirical validation in the semiconductor industry - characterized by tight geopolitical sensitivity and high ESG demands - shows that i-SUP significantly reduces disruptions and ESG incidents relative to static or cost-only methods. Moreover, ablation analyses confirm that removing any single module (GPT, BBN, RL, or NSGA-II) undermines performance, demonstrating the necessity of a fully integrated pipeline. The findings underscore i-SUP's ability to enhance supplier resilience and sustainability in a wide range of globalized supply networks that face evolving textual risk signals and multi-dimensional objectives. ESG trade-offs, enabling boards and procurement leaders to align operational policies with strategic sustainability goals. Overall, this paper presents a policy-relevant approach that informs how top management can leverage artificial intelligence in day-to-day supplier decisions and long-term contract negotiations, thereby advancing both financial outcomes and corporate social responsibility in high-stakes, rapidly changing supply networks.  © IEEE. 1988-2012 IEEE.},
